.TH LIBB 3 "2026-10-19" "libb 0.1.0" "42 Projects Manual"
.SH NAME
libb \- B language runtime and standard library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "/* Declaration in B source files */"
.nf
.B extrn char, lchar, putchar, getchar, printn, printf, ctime;
.B extrn read, write, open, close, creat, seek, unlink;
.B extrn chdir, chmod, chown, mkdir, link, stat, fstat;
.B extrn fork, wait, execl, execv, exit, getuid, setuid, time;
.fi
.PP
.SS Character and String I/O
.nf
.BI "c = char(" "string" ", " "i" ");"
.BI "lchar(" "string" ", " "i" ", " "c" ");"
.BI "putchar(" "c" ");"
.BI "c = getchar();"
.BI "printn(" "number" ", " "base" ");"
.BI "printf(" "format" ", " "arg1" ", ...);"
.BI "ctime(" "time" ", " "date" ");"
.fi
.SS File and I/O Operations
.nf
.BI "nread = read(" "file" ", " "buffer" ", " "count" ");"
.BI "nwrite = write(" "file" ", " "buffer" ", " "count" ");"
.BI "file = open(" "string" ", " "mode" ");"
.BI "error = close(" "file" ");"
.BI "file = creat(" "string" ", " "mode" ");"
.BI "error = seek(" "file" ", " "offset" ", " "pointer" ");"
.BI "error = unlink(" "string" ");"
.fi
.SS File System and Attributes
.nf
.BI "error = chdir(" "string" ");"
.BI "error = chmod(" "string" ", " "mode" ");"
.BI "error = chown(" "string" ", " "owner" ");"
.BI "error = mkdir(" "string" ", " "mode" ");"
.BI "error = link(" "string1" ", " "string2" ");"
.BI "error = stat(" "string" ", " "status" ");"
.BI "error = fstat(" "file" ", " "status" ");"
.fi
.SS Process Control and System
.nf
.BI "id = getuid();"
.BI "error = setuid(" "id" ");"
.BI "time(" "timev" ");"
.BI "error = fork();"
.BI "error = wait();"
.BI "execl(" "string" ", " "arg0" ", ..., 0);"
.BI "execv(" "string" ", " "argv" ", " "count" ");"
.BI "exit();"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B libb
library provides the standard runtime routines and UNIX system call wrappers for
programs compiled with the B programming language compiler, conforming to Ken
Thompson's
.I "Users' Reference to B"
(1972).
.PP
In B, all variables, parameters, and function return values are untyped 32-bit machine
words. Pointers refer to memory addresses, while strings are arrays of byte-packed
characters terminated by a null byte.
.PP
The functions in
.B libb
are categorized as follows:
.SS Character and String I/O
.TP
.BI "char(" "string" ", " "i" ")"
Returns the
.IR i -th
character (byte) from
.IR string .
Since standard B vector indexing (\fIa[b]\fR) operates on 32-bit machine words
(\fI*(a + 4*b)\fR),
.B char
is used to index individual bytes within character strings.
.TP
.BI "lchar(" "string" ", " "i" ", " "c" ")"
Stores the character
.I c
into the
.IR i -th
character (byte) position of
.IR string .
.TP
.BI "putchar(" "c" ")"
Writes the single character
.I c
to standard output (file descriptor 1). Returns 0 on success, or negative on error.
.TP
.BI "getchar()"
Reads the next character from standard input (file descriptor 0). Returns the
character read (as an unsigned byte value), or \-1 on end-of-file.
.TP
.BI "printn(" "number" ", " "base" ")"
Recursively prints the non-negative integer
.I number
converted into radix
.I base
(where 2 <= \fIbase\fR <= 10) to standard output.
.TP
.BI "printf(" "format" ", " "arg1" ", ...)"
Prints formatted output to standard output according to the format string
.IR format .
Format conversion specifiers begin with
.B %
and format the succeeding arguments in order:
.RS
.TP
.B %d
Prints the argument as a signed decimal integer.
.TP
.B %o
Prints the argument as an octal integer.
.TP
.B %s
Prints the argument as a null-terminated string.
.TP
.B %c
Prints the argument as a single character.
.TP
.B %%
Prints a literal percent sign.
.RE
.IP
All other characters are printed verbatim to standard output.
.TP
.BI "ctime(" "time" ", " "date" ")"
Converts the system timestamp stored in the vector
.I time
into a 16-character date string stored in vector
.IR date .
The formatted string follows the format:
.B "Mmm dd hh:mm:ss" .
.SS File and I/O Operations
.TP
.BI "read(" "file" ", " "buffer" ", " "count" ")"
Reads up to
.I count
bytes from open file descriptor
.I file
into memory starting at
.IR buffer .
Returns the number of bytes read, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "write(" "file" ", " "buffer" ", " "count" ")"
Writes
.I count
bytes from memory starting at
.I buffer
to open file descriptor
.IR file .
Returns the number of bytes written, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "open(" "string" ", " "mode" ")"
Opens the file at pathname
.IR string .
If
.I mode
is 0, the file is opened for reading (\fBO_RDONLY\fR); if non-zero, it is opened
for writing (\fBO_WRONLY\fR). Returns a non-negative file descriptor on success,
or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "close(" "file" ")"
Closes the open file descriptor
.IR file .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "creat(" "string" ", " "mode" ")"
Creates a new file or truncates an existing file specified by
.I string
with file permissions
.IR mode .
The file is opened for writing and its descriptor is returned. Returns a negative
value on error.
.TP
.BI "seek(" "file" ", " "offset" ", " "pointer" ")"
Repositions the read/write offset of open file descriptor
.I file
by
.I offset
bytes relative to
.IR pointer :
.RS
.TP
.B 0
Relative to the beginning of the file (\fBSEEK_SET\fR).
.TP
.B 1
Relative to the current file position (\fBSEEK_CUR\fR).
.TP
.B 2
Relative to the end of the file (\fBSEEK_END\fR).
.RE
.IP
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "unlink(" "string" ")"
Deletes the file name
.I string
from the filesystem. Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.SS File System and Attributes
.TP
.BI "chdir(" "string" ")"
Changes the current working directory to pathname
.IR string .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "chmod(" "string" ", " "mode" ")"
Changes the permissions of file
.I string
to octal mode
.IR mode .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "chown(" "string" ", " "owner" ")"
Changes the user ownership of file
.I string
to user ID
.IR owner .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "mkdir(" "string" ", " "mode" ")"
Creates a new directory named
.I string
with permissions
.IR mode .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "link(" "string1" ", " "string2" ")"
Creates a new hard link named
.I string2
pointing to existing file
.IR string1 .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "stat(" "string" ", " "status" ")"
Retrieves file status information for the file named by pathname
.I string
and stores it into vector
.IR status .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "fstat(" "file" ", " "status" ")"
Retrieves file status information for the open file descriptor
.I file
and stores it into vector
.IR status .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.SS Process Control and System
.TP
.BI "fork()"
Creates a new child process by duplicating the calling process. Returns 0 in the
child process, the child's process ID in the parent process, or a negative value
on failure.
.TP
.BI "wait()"
Suspends the calling process until one of its child processes terminates. Returns
the process ID of the terminated child, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "execl(" "string" ", " "arg0" ", ..., 0)"
Executes the binary file at pathname
.IR string ,
passing arguments
.I arg0
through the final 0 (null terminator). Replaces the calling process; only
returns on error (negative value).
.TP
.BI "execv(" "string" ", " "argv" ", " "count" ")"
Executes the binary file at pathname
.IR string ,
passing an array of
.I count
string pointers from vector
.IR argv .
Replaces the calling process; only returns on error (negative value).
.TP
.BI "exit()"
Terminates the current process immediately with exit status 0. Does not return.
.TP
.BI "getuid()"
Returns the real user ID of the calling process.
.TP
.BI "setuid(" "id" ")"
Sets the user ID of the calling process to
.IR id .
Returns 0 on success, or a negative value on error.
.TP
.BI "time(" "timev" ")"
Obtains the current system time in seconds since the UNIX Epoch (January 1, 1970)
and writes it into the vector
.IR timev .
Returns the timestamp value.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Unless specified otherwise, functions that return a status code return 0 or a
positive value on success, and a negative value on failure.
.SH NOTES
In accordance with project specifications, the historic PDP-11 teletype functions
.B gtty
and
.B stty
are omitted from this library implementation.
.SH EXAMPLES
A simple program printing to standard output and iterating over a string:
.PP
.nf
main() {
  extrn putchar, char, printf;
  auto s, i;

  s = "Hello from B!\en";
  i = 0;
  while (char(s, i)) {
    putchar(char(s, i));
    i++;
  }

  printf("Formatted number: %d (octal: %o)\en", 42, 42);
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR B (1),
.BR syscall (2),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR open (2),
.BR printf (3)
.SH AUTHORS
Written for the 42 B compiler project, based on Ken Thompson's original 1972 specification.
